When you first learn Python, you use the basic built-in data types. You use `str` for text, `int` for whole numbers, and `list` to hold multiple items. These basic types are great for simple programs.

But as your programs grow larger, you will need to group different pieces of information together.

To make your code safer and easier to read, you can create your own **custom data types**. By doing this, you tell Python exactly what information is allowed and how it should behave.

## When to Use Custom Types

You do not always need custom data types. Knowing when to use the built-in types (like lists and dictionaries) saves you a lot of time.

**Use Built-in Data Types When:**

- You are writing a quick, short script.
- The data is simple and temporary.
- **Example:** If you just need to read a text file and count how many times the word "Python" appears, you only need built-in strings (`str`) and numbers (`int`).

**Use Custom Data Types When:**

- You are building a large application that you will maintain for a long time.
- Specific pieces of data always travel together.
- The data has strict rules.
- **Example:** If you are building an email application, you should create a custom `Email` data type. An email must always have a sender, a receiver, a subject line, and a true/false value showing if it has been read. If you just use a dictionary, another programmer might forget to include the "sender" information, which would break the application. A custom type prevents this mistake.

Here are three ways to create custom data types in Python, starting with the most common way.

## 1. Using Standard Classes (For Data and Actions)

In Python, the most common way to create a custom data type is by using a `class`.

You can think of a class as a strict blueprint. It tells Python exactly what pieces of information belong together. It also lets you create actions that the data can perform.

{% codeEditor
   language="python"
   code="# We use the 'class' keyword to define our new custom data type named 'Player'\nclass Player:\n    \n    # The __init__ function is the setup function. \n    # It runs automatically every time you create a new Player.\n    # 'self' refers to the specific player object being created right now.\n    # 'name' and 'health' are the starting values we will provide.\n    def __init__(self, name, health):\n        # We take the 'name' provided and store it inside this player\n        self.name = name\n        # We take the 'health' provided and store it inside this player\n        self.health = health\n        \n    # This is an action (called a method) that only a Player can do.\n    # We must include 'self' so the function knows which player is taking damage.\n    def take_damage(self, amount):\n        # We subtract the damage amount from this player's current health\n        self.health = self.health - amount\n        print(f\"{self.name} has {self.health} health left.\")\n\n# Now we create a real piece of data using our new custom type.\n# We pass \"Sam\" as the name, and 100 as the health.\nplayer_one = Player(\"Sam\", 100)\n\n# We can tell our player to perform its specific action\nplayer_one.take_damage(20)\n\n# If we check the type, Python knows it is our custom Player type\nprint(type(player_one))" /%}

## 2. Using Data Classes (For Just Holding Information)

Writing the setup function (the `__init__` part in the code above) can take a lot of time if you have 10 or 20 pieces of information to store.

If you want to create a custom data type that only holds information and does not need to perform actions, you should use a **Data Class**. Python added Data Classes to write all the repetitive setup code for you behind the scenes.

{% codeEditor
   language="python"
   code="# We must import 'dataclass' from Python's built-in tools before we can use it\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\n\n# The @dataclass label tells Python to automatically create the setup code for us\n@dataclass\nclass Product:\n    # We just list the names of the variables and what built-in type they should be.\n    # Python handles the rest.\n    name: str\n    price: float\n    \n    # We can also set a default starting value.\n    # If we don't specify otherwise, a product will always start as True for in_stock.\n    in_stock: bool = True  \n\n# Creating a new product is simple and clean. \n# We only need to provide the name and the price.\nmy_item = Product(\"Laptop\", 999.50)\n\n# Data classes also automatically make the data look nice when you print it.\n# It will print exactly what information is stored inside.\nprint(my_item)" /%}

## 3. Using Enums (For a Strict List of Choices)

Sometimes, a piece of data should only be allowed to be one of a few exact choices.

For example, an order status in a store application should only be "Pending", "Shipped", or "Delivered". If you use regular text strings for this, someone might accidentally type "Shiped" with one "p".

To prevent these spelling mistakes, you can use an `Enum`. This simply means a numbered list of specific choices.

{% codeEditor
   language="python"
   code="# We must import 'Enum' from Python's built-in tools\nfrom enum import Enum\n\n# We create our custom type by putting '(Enum)' after the name\nclass OrderStatus(Enum):\n    # We list the only choices that are allowed.\n    # It is standard practice to write Enum choices in ALL CAPS.\n    PENDING = 1\n    SHIPPED = 2\n    DELIVERED = 3\n\n# We can now set a variable using one of our strict choices\ncurrent_status = OrderStatus.SHIPPED\n\n# If we try to type OrderStatus.SHIPED (with one 'P'), \n# Python will show an error before the program even runs. This keeps the code safe.\n\n# Python recognizes this as our custom OrderStatus type\nprint(type(current_status))" /%}

## Summary

Here is a simple guide to help you choose which method to use:

{% table %}
- What do you need your custom data type to do?
- What should you use?
---
- It needs to hold information **and** perform specific actions.
- Use a standard `class`.
---
- It only needs to hold information.
- Use a `@dataclass`.
---
- It needs to restrict information to a specific list of choices.
- Use an `Enum`.
{% /table %}
